          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.071,
            0.593,
            0.097
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.6 Floating and Sinking"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.122,
            0.576,
            0.209
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If we place some objects on water, some of them float, while others fall to the bottom. The gravitational force of the Earth is acting on all objects, then why don’t all objects fall to the bottom?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.59,
            0.113,
            0.883,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "While taking out water from a bucket filled with water using a mug, do you notice that the mug feels lighter when it is inside water? Let us try to "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "understand",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " this."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.269,
            0.242,
            0.687,
            0.266
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 5.13: Let us investigate"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.279,
            0.882,
            0.316
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Take an empty bottle (with its lid closed tightly) and a bucket full of water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.069,
            0.332,
            0.235,
            0.474
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.069,
                0.332,
                0.235,
                0.474
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_14_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.081,
                0.48,
                0.229,
                0.51
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.15: Bottle in water"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.318,
            0.882,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Push the bottle in the water (Fig. 5.15). Do you feel an upward push? Release the bottle. Does it bounce up?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.358,
            0.883,
            0.451
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You would have felt an upward push and the bottle bounces back to the surface of the water. This indicates that water applies a force on the bottle in the upward direction. In fact, all liquids apply a similar force. The force applied by a liquid on an object in the upward direction is known as "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "upthrust",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "buoyant force",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.452,
            0.883,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When an object is placed in a liquid, the gravitational force due to the Earth acts on it downwards. But a buoyant force is applied on it by the liquid in the upward direction. If the gravitational force is more than the buoyant force, the object "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "sinks",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", but if the two forces are equal, the object "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "floats",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". One of the factors on which the buoyant force depends upon, is the density of the liquid. You will learn about density in a later chapter of this book."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.09,
            0.623,
            0.259,
            0.752
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.09,
                0.623,
                0.259,
                0.752
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_14_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.594,
            0.437,
            0.615
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A step further"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.627,
            0.863,
            0.75
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Archimedes, a famous Greek scientist, discovered that when an object is fully or partially immersed in a liquid, it experiences an upward force which is equal to the weight of the liquid it displaces. This is known as Archimedes’ Principle. If the weight of a liquid displaced by an object is smaller than the weight of the object, the object will sink in the liquid. If the weight of the liquid displaced is equal to the weight of the object, the object will float in the liquid."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.271,
            0.769,
            0.451,
            0.79
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ever heard of ..."
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.191,
            0.829,
            0.266,
            0.914
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 15,
              "bbox": [
                0.191,
                0.829,
                0.266,
                0.914
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_14_image_15.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.802,
            0.737,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There are some rocks which can float on water. One such rock is Pumice, which is formed during volcanic eruptions. When lava with lots of gas and water vapour cools quickly, it traps tiny bubbles of gas inside. This creates a light, porous rock — filled"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.744,
            0.808,
            0.864,
            0.869
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 17,
              "bbox": [
                0.744,
                0.808,
                0.864,
                0.869
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_14_image_17.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.887,
            0.835,
            0.906
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "with air pockets which is less dense than water and floats on it."
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
